  "notes": "British interpolation-first dance-pop — tropical/deep-house arrangements built around a re-recorded classic hook. The tempo is catalogue-sampled: readings of 124 / 124 / 125 / 125 / 125 / 125 / 120 / 120, \"120-125, sitting on 124-125\", so the mode is 124. The harmonic profile is the most functional in the tropical block and the research says why: \"because the source material is usually 60s-90s soul or pop, the harmony carries real functional motion and 7th/9th extensions rather than the static minor loop of the EDM field. Trained pianist (from age 8, to grade 8) with a commercial-music degree, which is the reason the chords move.\" That is five families with ii-V motion, a 0.30/0.44/0.26 extension spread and a `1_per_bar` harmonic rhythm. The groove is written to sit under a soul vocal: \"kick is round rather than clipped; the groove is meant to swing\", hence a 0.54 swing and a live-feel percussion bed of shaker, tambourine and congas/bongos on 16ths at 5-8 hits a bar. The bass \"walks between chord tones in the soul manner rather than sitting on the root\" — a 0.45 root probability on offbeat 8ths. The identifying method, an interpolation re-recorded by session players and singers rather than sampled, then treated as the instrumental hook, is why the arrangement puts an instrumental break after the chorus. ⚠ The drum detail is convention-derived by the entry's own statement; the velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
